<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Shah III of Johor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1756–1812</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Shah III was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riau">Riau</a> from 1761 to 1812, succeeding as a child. Power at his court lay largely with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> viceroys, whose war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> led to the Dutch occupation of Riau in 1784. He later moved his court to Lingga. His death in 1812 left a disputed succession between his sons Hussein and Abdul Rahman, which the British exploited in founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in 1819.</p></div>
